fact WorkItem(id: "WI-750", description: some(value: "Converter+loader: a METHOD CALL whose receiver is a rule-reference CHAIN ('person_row.negate.takeN(5)', 'Queen.find.negate.where(lambda ...)') is not re-routed and fails loudly as 'unknown functor'. CONTEXT: found by the /code-review pass on WI-749 (2026-07-18), reproduced by four independent reviewers. WI-749 widened the ZERO-ARG field path to admit a receiver whose name has a PREFIX resolving to a rule, so member chains on a relation now re-route level by level ('person_row.negate.isEmpty' LOADS and evals equal to its let-bound spelling). The METHOD-CALL path was NOT widened and still reaches a rule receiver only at DEPTH 1: dot_call_receiver probes the WHOLE receiver name only. So the same receiver is a VALUE for 'x.m' and a static NAME PATH for 'x.m(args)' -- 'person_row.negate.isEmpty' works but 'person_row.negate.takeN(5)' does not. NOT a WI-749 regression (both spellings failed before it); WI-749 moved the field boundary without moving the call side. WHY IT MATTERS: takeN is the standard way to DRAIN a relation, so it is the first thing tried after the inline projection works; and it breaks the flagship 052 algebra chain 'person_row.negate.where(lambda c -> eq(c.name, \\\"alice\\\")).isEmpty', which fails with the exact WI-723 symptom ('<unresolved receiver>.name' -- the row lambda gets no schema). ROOT CAUSE (known precisely): parse/convert.rs push_fn_term flattens a dotted call CALLEE into ONE dotted functor symbol, destroying the receiver's parse node, whereas the 'name' arm keeps a dotted NAME as a nested field_access chain. So load.rs dot_call_receiver must return ONE Symbol to synthesize mk_var_ref(receiver_sym) and has no parse node to Visit -- structurally unable to express a chained receiver. This is the SAME shape as WI-443's deferred chained-LOCAL receiver, still open on both paths ('p.x.isEmpty' works, 'p.x.startsWith(...)' fails). FIX DIRECTION (preferred = b): (a) synthesize a NESTED DotApply receiver on the call path, lifting WI-443's deferral; (b) DEEPER: stop the converter flattening a dotted call callee -- have push_fn_term build the same structured field_access receiver the 'name' arm already builds, so BOTH paths share ONE structural receiver classifier and the loader stops maintaining two that must be diffed by eye (this asymmetry is the only reason two exist); (c) narrow the WI-749 field widening to the whole receiver name -- REJECTED: regresses WI-749 depth-2 and makes rule receivers behave unlike local ones. Prefer (b): it also closes WI-443's local chain and removes the drift hazard that produced WI-729 and WI-749. ACCEPTANCE: 'person_row.negate.takeN(5)', 'person_row.negate.where(lambda c -> ...)' and the full chain 'person_row.negate.where(lambda ...).isEmpty' load, type and EVAL equal to their let-bound spellings; the row lambda binder types at the CONCRETE schema through a chained receiver (the WI-729 discriminator, not an absorbing type var); if fixed via (b), the local chain 'p.x.startsWith(...)' works too; a receiver naming neither a local nor a rule stays the loud unknown-functor error (no silent fallback); WI-714/WI-723/WI-729/WI-749 suites unaffected; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-749"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-07-18T11:01:24Z"))
